# LayerFS × DSH

LayerFS×and![](/integrations/dsh.svg)DSH

`dsh-layerfs` is a plugin bundle for [DeepSeek Harness](https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness) (dsh). Agents in a dsh session work on a LayerFS project from bash, through short Node scripts that use the JavaScript SDK: they fork Branches, test them in Sandboxes, and merge and publish. You watch every agent's Branch grow, live. It changes no dsh code.

previewLayerFS JS SDK 1.0.0 · dsh webdesign: issue #346

![](/agents/crab.png)![](/agents/jelly.png)![](/agents/ghost.png)![](/agents/frog.png)

[video](/integrations/dsh-layerfs.mp4)

*A recorded run: a lead and five agents build one application on Branches of their own. Work shows their terminals; History grows as they commit, merge and publish.*

## Install

The quickest way is to let a coding agent do it. Paste this into one running on the machine where dsh web will run, in a folder holding a clone of the [dsh-layerfs repository](https://github.com/Ephemeral-AI-Lab/dsh-layerfs):

![](/agents/cloud.png)Install prompt for your agent Copy prompt

```
Install LayerFS × DSH 0.1.0 for me: the dsh-layerfs plugin in DeepSeek Harness (dsh) web, on real LayerFS v1.1.0, then run its scenario tests in dsh web on the default profile at http://127.0.0.1:3080 and tell me the results.

This machine is macOS, Linux or Windows: find out which, follow the steps for it and skip the others. This is an install-and-test run: change no code in any checkout. When something fails, find the cause from the source and bounded output, tell me with a proposed fix, and go on where you can.

Rules:
- Pin exactly: https://github.com/Ephemeral-AI-Lab/dsh-layerfs at commit baac1b92b97f0bd492002c285f6a13401b4f4c0b, and https://github.com/Ephemeral-AI-Lab/layerfs at commit 9f97a7ec95b662e9cb288704afa165feff132cfa. The plugin's layerfs.json names the same LayerFS commit. Read AGENTS.md in both checkouts first.
- Give every test command a wall timeout of at most 120 s, and keep every other command under 2 minutes: put timeout 110 before long steps (macOS has no timeout; use perl -e 'alarm 110; exec @ARGV' <command>) and run a long build again until it finishes, since Cargo keeps what it compiled.
- One attempt per check: no retry loops, and no reruns to turn a failure into a pass.
- Stores live on a local Linux filesystem (ext4, xfs or btrfs). On Windows never put a Store or the Linux LayerFS home on /mnt/c or any Windows-backed mount.
- Use dsh's default web profile: no DSH_HOME override. Before changing it, copy its cordis.patch.yml and package.json to a dated backup folder, and keep its existing patch entries. Never restart a dsh web that has a run in progress.
- Do not commit, push, delete other volumes or containers, or install system-wide software without asking me.

Install:
1. Prerequisites; record each version. All: Node 22.19 or newer, pnpm, python3, Docker, network access, and a built deepseek-harness checkout (https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness) beside the plugin, with a dsh version inside the plugin's packages/dsh-layerfs/package.json engines.dsh; if there is none, clone it there and run pnpm install && pnpm run build in it. macOS: Docker Desktop running. Linux: a Docker Engine, /dev/fuse, and a Store filesystem that is ext4, xfs or btrfs. Windows: WSL2 with a selected distribution (default Ubuntu) that has /dev/fuse, its own running Docker Engine (not Docker Desktop's Windows pipe), and Node 22.19 or newer at an absolute path. Keep at least 20 GiB free inside that Docker Engine. If something is missing that you cannot install, stop and tell me which.
2. LayerFS checkouts at 9f97a7ec95b662e9cb288704afa165feff132cfa: one on macOS or Linux; on Windows two of the same commit, one in WSL for the sealed bundle and one on Windows for the dsh Host (record core.autocrlf: the worker refuses a Windows SDK that differs from the bundle's, apart from line endings). In the checkout the Host uses, run npm install in api and npm --prefix api/agent/cli install; they only link packages.
3. Runtime image: build it from the plugin's tools/runtime/Dockerfile in the Docker Engine the worker and Sandboxes use (on Windows, the WSL one), and record its sha256 image ID. Check it has cargo (docker run --rm <image> cargo --version): the Depot scenario needs it.
4. LayerFS build. macOS and Windows: build the sealed Linux bundle with the LayerFS checkout's tools/worktree.py (register, prepare, build --platform linux --profile release; see docs/general/parallel-worktrees.md), on Windows inside WSL; record the printed build-linux-… folder and its manifest.json hash, and confirm it holds api/runtime/src/worker/server.js, layerfs-daemon and layerfs.node. Linux: build layerfs-daemon and the addon (the plugin README's step 1 recipe, or npm --prefix api run build:native), and record the daemon path and the layerfs.node dsh web will load.
5. Plugin: in the dsh-layerfs checkout, with LAYERFS_SOURCE set to the Host's LayerFS checkout, run pnpm install, then pnpm run link:layerfs, then pnpm run build with DSH_SOURCE set to the deepseek-harness checkout (absolute paths). pnpm run check:layerfs must report the checkout "at the pinned commit".
6. Plugin tests, each bounded at 120 s: pnpm test and pnpm run test:scenarios; on Windows also pnpm run test:windows.
7. Profile: add the plugin to the default web profile as the plugin README's steps 3 and 4 describe, but without the DSH_HOME override. Its layerfs-host entry in cordis.patch.yml takes: image (your sha256, on Windows from the WSL Engine); bundle (macOS: the sealed bundle; Windows: its WSL path); wslDistribution (Windows only); daemon (Linux, required; elsewhere it defaults to the bundle's layerfs-daemon); volumes (Linux: Docker's volumes directory, which dsh web's user must be able to write). Keep containerPrefix at its default unless something else in that Docker Engine already uses layerfs- containers. On Linux, run dsh web as a user that can write Docker's volumes directory (such as root), with a home that user owns, or Init fails Unsupported.
8. Start dsh web with the Node from step 1 (a login shell may pick an older one): pnpm --dir <deepseek-harness> dsh web --no-open --port 3080. GET /layerfs/status must report an empty missing list. Give me the printed http://127.0.0.1:3080/?token=… link.
9. On the LayerFS page, open All projects: Docker must be green, with host and Docker free space shown. On macOS and Windows "Store calls · worker ready" must be green; if it is not, press its Start button once and tell me what happened.

Run the scenario tests:
10. Open the LayerFS page's Tests tab. It runs the LayerFS scripted model, which ships switched on: scripted agent runs through the real dsh agent loop, with no language model or API key. Press Run all once; it runs every scenario, up to 2 at once, five through the SDK and five through the layerfs command. Most take under a minute, Depot and Crew several minutes each; the whole run takes about 15 to 20 minutes. Don't press Run again while it is going.
11. When every row has finished, tell me each scenario's result and time, and for a failure the reason its row shows. Check a failed run's session with node tools/checks/run.mjs <profile dir> <root session id> in the dsh-layerfs checkout, bounded at 120 s, and leave its project as it is.

Report every step with its command and outcome (PASS, FAIL, or NOT_RUN with the reason), the versions, the image ID, the bundle folder or daemon path, and the Tests tab table. If a step fails, stop and give me the exact error unless a rule above says to go on; don't retry it on your own.
```

It follows the six steps below and asks before anything it cannot do. Expect a few minutes for the first LayerFS build.

**▶Install step by stepsix steps, without an agent**

You need Node 22.19 or newer and pnpm; a built [deepseek-harness](https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness) checkout (its own README: `pnpm install && pnpm run build`); Docker (Docker Desktop on macOS); and network access for step 1. Below, `$DSH_LAYERFS` is the dsh-layerfs clone's absolute path, `$LAYERFS_SOURCE` a[LayerFS](https://github.com/Ephemeral-AI-Lab/layerfs) checkout at the commit in its `layerfs.json`, and `$DSH` the deepseek-harness checkout's. The full guide, with every setting, is the [dsh-layerfs README](https://github.com/Ephemeral-AI-Lab/dsh-layerfs#readme).

### 1. Install LayerFS

Nothing is published yet, so build a Linux `layerfs-daemon` and the SDK's native addon in the Rust 1.85.1 image, from the repository root. A cold release build takes a few minutes; if it stops, run it again and Cargo keeps what it compiled.

```
cd "$LAYERFS_SOURCE"
docker pull rust:1.85.1-bookworm
docker volume create layerfs-cargo && docker volume create layerfs-target
mkdir -p core/target/cluster2-linux
docker run --rm -v "$LAYERFS_SOURCE":/work:ro -v layerfs-cargo:/usr/local/cargo/registry -w /work \
  rust:1.85.1-bookworm cargo fetch --manifest-path core/Cargo.toml --locked
docker run --rm -v "$LAYERFS_SOURCE":/work:ro -v layerfs-target:/work/core/target/cluster2-linux \
  -v layerfs-cargo:/usr/local/cargo/registry:ro -e CARGO_TARGET_DIR=/work/core/target/cluster2-linux -w /work \
  rust:1.85.1-bookworm cargo build --manifest-path core/Cargo.toml --offline --locked --release -j 6 \
  -p layerfs-node --lib -p layerfs-daemon --bins
```

Gather the build into one folder, the bundle (any folder Docker Desktop shares):

```
BUNDLE=$HOME/layerfs-linux-build
mkdir -p "$BUNDLE/api/agent"
docker run --rm -v layerfs-target:/t:ro -v "$BUNDLE":/out rust:1.85.1-bookworm sh -c \
  "cp /t/release/layerfs-daemon /out/ && cp /t/release/liblayerfs_node.so /out/layerfs.node && chown $(id -u):$(id -g) /out/layerfs-daemon /out/layerfs.node"
cp -R api/package.json api/backend api/runtime "$BUNDLE/api/"
cp -R api/agent/sdk "$BUNDLE/api/agent/"
```

That folder serves Linux. **macOS and Windows need a sealed bundle**: the worker checks every file against the folder's `manifest.json` before it listens, and only LayerFS's `tools/worktree.py build` writes one. The guide has its three commands.

Then build the runtime image that Sandboxes and the macOS worker run, and note its ID:

```
docker build -t layerfs-352-runtime:node24 "$DSH_LAYERFS/tools/runtime"
docker image inspect --format '{{.Id}}' layerfs-352-runtime:node24     # the image setting
```

Agents' Bash calls use the [`layerfs` command](/docs/sdk/agent/#layerfs), the checkout's`api/agent/cli/` folder. Link it and the packages once; neither command downloads anything:

```
npm --prefix "$LAYERFS_SOURCE/api" install              # links the three packages to each other
npm --prefix "$LAYERFS_SOURCE/api/agent/cli" install    # links the layerfs command to @layerfs/agent
```

### 2. Build the dsh bundle

```
cd "$DSH_LAYERFS"
LAYERFS_SOURCE="$LAYERFS_SOURCE" pnpm install
DSH_SOURCE="$DSH" pnpm run build
```

### 3. Make a profile

Use a `DSH_HOME` of your own, so your usual `~/.dsh` is not touched. dsh's`plugin` command makes the `web` profile and adds the bundle to it:

```
export DSH_HOME=$HOME/dsh-layerfs        # any empty folder
pnpm --dir "$DSH" dsh plugin --profile web add "link:$DSH_LAYERFS/packages/dsh-layerfs"
```

Install `dsh-layerfs` only; the demo bundle is not part of the release.

### 4. Configure Live

dsh writes the profile's `cordis.patch.yml` as `[]`; replace it with the Host's settings. The image is required everywhere, the bundle on macOS and the daemon on Linux:

```
# $DSH_HOME/profiles/web/cordis.patch.yml
- id: layerfs-host
  config:
    image: "sha256:<64 hex>"                   # the runtime image ID
    bundle: /Users/<you>/layerfs-linux-build    # macOS: the bundle folder
    # daemon: /path/to/layerfs-daemon           # Linux
```

Keep the Host's `home` and `traceDir` in the OS temp area (the default): bash calls in Workspace Write mode may only write there. Until the required settings are in, Init is refused with "LayerFS is not configured", and `GET /layerfs/status` lists what is missing.

### 5. Start dsh web

```
DSH_HOME=$HOME/dsh-layerfs pnpm --dir "$DSH" dsh web --no-open --port 3090
```

Open the printed `http://127.0.0.1:3090/?token=…` link.

### 6. Check

```
cd "$DSH_LAYERFS" && pnpm test
```

It runs the Host, simulator, model, playback and history tests.

## Two real runs

On 2026-10-20, one dsh main agent and seven worker subagents on `deepseek-flash` took an empty LayerFS project to a published, verified `main`, twice: a small app in under an hour and a hard one in an afternoon. Every change went through the SDK as Branches, Commits, merges and publishes.

